    standardId: 'pec', standardName: 'Philippine Electrical Code Part 1', edition,
    reference: 'PEC Part 1 Article 2.30, especially §§2.30.1.2, 2.30.4.3, 2.30.5.5, 2.30.6.1-.10, and 2.30.7.1-.6',
    references: ['§§2.30.1.1-.2 - scope and number of services (supplied PDF pp. 75, 81)', '§2.30.4.3 - service-entrance conductor ampacity (supplied PDF p. 84)', '§§2.30.5.1, 2.30.5.5 - guarding and suitable-for-service-equipment marking (supplied PDF p. 87)', '§§2.30.6.1-.10 - service disconnecting means (supplied PDF pp. 87-89)', '§§2.30.7.1-.6 - service overcurrent and ground-fault protection (supplied PDF pp. 89-90)', '§2.50.2.5 - service grounding and main bonding (supplied PDF pp. 113-115)', '§1.10.1.9 - interrupting rating (supplied PDF p. 20)'],
    referenceDetails: { article: '2.30', section: '2.30.1.1-.2; 2.30.4.3; 2.30.5.1, .5; 2.30.6.1-.10; 2.30.7.1-.6' },
    quickAnswer: 'Begin at the point of service and map every conductor and disconnect. Verify whether more than one service is permitted, calculate service-entrance conductor ampacity, confirm service equipment is marked suitable, locate and group the disconnects, enforce the applicable minimum rating, coordinate overcurrent and ground-fault protection, and complete service grounding, bonding, available-fault-current, and working-space checks.',
    summary: `Article 2.30 covers service conductors and equipment from the service point into the premises wiring system. A compliant design begins with a one-line diagram that clearly identifies the service point, service drop or lateral, service-entrance conductors, metering, service disconnecting means, overcurrent protection, grounded conductor, grounding electrode conductor, main bonding point, and downstream feeders. Without those boundaries, it is easy to apply feeder rules on the line side of the service disconnect or to place grounding and bonding connections at the wrong location.

Section 2.30.1.2 generally limits a building or other structure to one service. It then recognizes stated conditions for additional services, including special conditions such as fire pumps, emergency systems, legally required standby systems, optional standby systems, parallel power production, and systems designed for enhanced reliability. Other provisions address special occupancies, capacity requirements, and different service characteristics. When multiple services are installed, permanent identification is required at each service disconnect to show the other services and their locations. Multiple services are therefore a controlled exception path, not an ordinary convenience.

Service-entrance conductor ampacity is governed by §2.30.4.3 together with the applicable load calculation. Before adjustment or correction, the general relationship is the noncontinuous load plus 125 percent of the continuous load. The section provides a 100-percent alternative only when the overcurrent assembly and conductors are listed for operation at 100 percent of rating. The calculated ampacity must also meet the service-disconnect rating provisions and any installation-specific minimum. The grounded conductor is sized under its separate rules and should not be assumed equal to an ungrounded conductor without analysis.

Service equipment must enclose or guard energized parts and, for equipment rated 600 V or less, must be marked suitable for use as service equipment. A meter socket by itself is not service equipment. The disconnect must open all service ungrounded conductors, be readily accessible, be outside or inside nearest the point of entrance of the service conductors, and not be installed in a bathroom. It must be permanently marked as a service disconnect. The Code generally permits no more than six disconnects for each service or permitted set and requires them to be grouped, subject to the detailed arrangement and special-system provisions.

The disconnect must have the appropriate rating. Section 2.30.6.10 states specific minimums: 15 A for a service supplying only one limited load circuit, 30 A for not more than two two-wire branch circuits, 100 A and three-wire for a one-family dwelling, and 60 A for other installations. These values do not replace the calculated load; the selected rating must satisfy both the calculation and the applicable minimum.

Section 2.30.7.1 requires overload protection in each ungrounded service conductor with a rating generally not exceeding conductor ampacity, subject to stated exceptions. The service overcurrent device must be integral with or immediately adjacent to the service disconnect. Ground-fault protection of equipment is required for a solidly grounded wye service above 150 V to ground but not over 600 V phase-to-phase when the service disconnect is rated 1000 A or more. The maximum setting is 1200 A and the maximum time delay is one second for ground-fault currents of 3000 A or more. The installation must be performance-tested and a written record supplied. Stated exceptions include specified continuous industrial processes and fire pumps.

Finally, service protection must match available fault current and equipment interrupting ratings. Grounding and bonding must establish the required grounded-conductor connection, main bonding jumper, grounding electrode conductor, and effective fault-current path at the service. Working clearance, weather exposure, labeling, accessibility, utility requirements, local rules, and authority-having-jurisdiction requirements complete the design.`,
