    workflows: [{ title: 'Service and service-equipment workflow', steps: ['Define utility/premises service point', 'Draw conductors, metering, disconnect, OCP, and bonding point', 'Confirm one-service rule or document permitted additional service', 'Calculate service load under Article 2.20', 'Classify continuous and noncontinuous portions', 'Calculate service-entrance ampacity', 'Select conductor arrangement and apply conditions of use', 'Confirm service equipment marking and guarding', 'Locate and identify service disconnect', 'Check grouping and maximum number of disconnects', 'Apply calculated and minimum disconnect rating', 'Provide overcurrent protection for ungrounded conductors', 'Screen for §2.30.7.6 GFPE', 'Calculate available fault current and verify interrupting rating', 'Design service grounding and main bonding', 'Verify working space, access, weather protection, and labels', 'Plan required performance tests and records', 'Coordinate utility, local rule, and AHJ requirements'], note: 'The service one-line should make every boundary and protection function visually explicit.' }],
    engineeringNotes: ['Why this matters: the service is the transition between utility supply and premises wiring, so a boundary error propagates into protection and grounding decisions.', 'Field consideration: utility service rules can add metering, enclosure, and conductor requirements without replacing PEC compliance.', 'Engineering recommendation: document available fault current and selected equipment ratings directly on the service one-line.'],
